<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Tamar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The River Tamar flows about 98 kilometres south from near the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plymouth-sound">Plymouth Sound</a>, forming most of the boundary between Cornwall and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a>. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a> is said to have fixed it as the border of the Cornish around 936, and it has long been seen as the line between Cornwall and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Brunel's Royal Albert Bridge crosses its estuary.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-tamar.jpg" alt="River Tamar" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roger Cornfoot · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:River_Tamar,_near_Bohetherick_-_geograph.org.uk_-_977730.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Tamar rises at Woolley Moor, about 5.6 kilometres from the north Cornish coast, and flows south to the Hamoaze, which opens into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plymouth-sound">Plymouth Sound</a>. Its tributaries include the Inny, Ottery, Kensey and Lynher on the Cornish side and the Deer and Tavy on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a> side. Ptolemy mentioned the name in the second century CE, and it is said to mean &quot;great water&quot;. The Ravenna Cosmography names a Roman settlement called Tamaris, though it is unclear which town along the river is meant.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> sailed up the Tamar and the Tavy in 997 as far as Lydford and burned the monastery at Tavistock. In the 11th century several villages north of Launceston passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a> when the border was drawn along the River Ottery instead of the Tamar, and the line was restored to the Tamar in 1966. In the English Civil War Cornwall was loyal to Charles I while Devon was mostly Parliamentarian, so each side needed the river crossings to invade or defend.</p><p>The valley was worked almost continuously for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>, lead and arsenic, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> to the modern era. Tavistock was one of the three stannary towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a>, and refined tin was exported through Morwellham from the 12th century until 1838. The royal mines on the Bere peninsula produced much lead and silver in the 13th century. In the 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> was shipped from Morwellham, Calstock and New Quay to south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> for smelting, and arsenic went to the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> as an insecticide for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>. Railways reached the valley from 1859, and traffic on the river fell as the mines were exhausted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The tidal river runs for 19.2 miles, to a weir near Gunnislake. It has 22 road crossings, the oldest surviving being the bridge at Horsebridge, dating from 1437. The Royal Albert Bridge of 1859 carries the railway between Saltash and Plymouth, beside the Tamar Bridge opened in 1962, and the Calstock Viaduct was built between 1904 and 1907. The lower valley is a National Landscape, designated in 1995, and was added to the World Heritage List in July 2006 as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a> and West Devon Mining Landscape.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “River Tamar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>David the Builder settled thousands of Kipchak families from the steppes in Georgia as soldiers, and <b>Tamar</b> was crowned with the title of king rather than queen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Marriage alliances supported this policy: Manuchihr III married <b>Tamar</b>, daughter of David IV of Georgia, and Georgian influence was strong at his court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvanshah State · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>By the early 8th century Wessex had taken Devon, and the British realm beyond the <b>Tamar</b> became known as Kernow or Cornwall.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-cornwall">Kingdom of Cornwall</a></li></ul></section>
